  after: [
    "let accepted names the original Right 2 and let rejected the original Left reason. either.swap moves Right 2 to Left 2, and the Left reason to Right with the same reason. The type changes from Either<String, Int> to Either<Int, String>.",
    "notification displays the integer in Left count using show, or the string in Right reason. The reason moved to the right still describes the original rejected input. Swapping does not mean the booking succeeded or recovered.",
    "pub effect fn main is the program entry. do runs the println steps from top to bottom to display strings. Function calls separate arguments with spaces. show converts a number to display text."
  ],
  comparisonBefore: "TS Result uses ok to distinguish a count and a reason. The notification consumer’s Notification uses kind with \"count\" or \"reason\". adapt moves the same information to that shape, and notification displays it.",
  comparisonAfter: "Both versions adapt the same information to a different consumer contract; this is not a claim that TS has built-in Left and Right. If no reversed existing contract needs adapting, ordinary match or if is more direct.",
  details: [
    "In swap<E, A>, E is the original left-side type and A the right-side type; the result is Either<A, E>. String and Int exchange positions here. No payload-transforming function is supplied.",
    "Applying swap twice restores the original alternatives. It neither validates an error nor reruns the step that returned Left. The common Left-failure/Right-success convention does not automatically describe this reversed notification consumer."
